## Results and Discussion
For this study we used the Protein Data Bank (PDB) Select April 2002 list of non-redundant protein chains (25% threshold version).\(^{13}\) Defining helices as described by Gunasekaran,\(^{14}\) this list contains 1739 chains from 1670 proteins, with a total of 8227 helices of at least five residues. Figure 1 illustrates the frequency of observation of helices of different lengths in the overall population. In agreement with earlier workers, we observe a peak near \(N=10\).\(^{15}\) Analogous to Doig's methods,\(^{16}\) the distribution is fit to a smooth equation. We found that a vertically shifted Gaussian provided a somewhat better fit than Doig's quartic expression. The difference between observed lengths and the Gaussian fit is expressed in the inset plot of weighted residuals \((w r_{i})\), defined in terms of the observed frequency \(f_{i}(\mathrm{obs})\)
and the Gaussian fit \( f_{i}(\mathrm{calc}) \) by equation (1):
\[
w_{fi}=\frac{f_{i}(\mathrm{obs})-f_{i}(\mathrm{calc})}{f_{i}(\mathrm{calc})}
\]
To further explore this periodicity, we investigated the relationship between position in a helix and the amino acid filling that position. Following
Kumar & Bansal,12 we define position-specific propensity \((P_{ij})\) to be:
\[
P_{ij}=\frac{f_{ij}}{f_{i}}=\frac{n_{ij}/\sum_{i}n_{ij}}{N_{i}/\sum_{i}N_{i}}
\]
where \(n_{i j}\) and \(f_{i j}\) are the number and fraction, respectively, of a given amino acid (type \(i)\) in helical position \(j,N_{i}\) and \(f_{i}\) are the number and fraction, respectively, of a given amino acid (type \(i)\) over the entire database irrespective of secondary structure.
We examined \( P_{ij} \) for the first seven, ten, and 15 positions from the N-cap in helices. Similar patterns in propensity were observed for all helix
lengths (not shown), so further investigations focused on helices of at least 15 residues. There were 2393 such helices within the database.
Figure 2 shows the average propensities within these helices for each amino acid, grouped by the physicochemical properties of the amino acid
Figure 2 shows the average propensities within these helices for each amino acid, grouped by the physicochemical properties of the amino acid side-chains. In this Figure we grouped the amino acids according to: long polar (Figure 2(a), E, K, Q, R) short polar residues (Figure 2(b), D, N, S), aromatics (Figure 2(c), F, W, Y), hydrophobic aliphatic residues plus Cys (Figure 2(d), C, I L, V, M), and others that do not fall easily into any one of these
categories (Figure 2(e), G, A, P, H, P, T). Previously, it was expected that the propensities would become independent of position, beyond approximately the N4 position.12 Instead, we find strong
and highly significant deviations from this isotropic expectation at all positions examined. This is one of the two most striking features of Figure 2. The other is its periodicity. We observe a repeat
of three to four residues, matching the geometric repeat of the \(\alpha\)-helix. As far as ten residues from the N terminus of the helix, this periodicity is still strongly apparent. Indeed, there is greater than a twofold difference in position-specific propensity
for many near-neighbor positions. For example, Asp at N11 has a propensity of 0.44 and at N13 its propensity is 0.98; the corresponding numbers for Glu are 0.78 and 1.41, respectively, and for Cys are 1.56 and 0.70. Weak periodicity in residue identity
is expected from earlier work.17 However, previous work was conducted on a much smaller database and compared only \( i \) to \( i+4 \), \( i+7 \), and \( i+11 \), while our analysis compares residues relative to their positions from helix ends.
In Figure 2, we observe that the phase of the distribution depends on the physicochemical properties of the amino acids. For example, polar residues tend to peak at N6, N9–N10, and N13, whereas non-polar residues are \( 180^{\circ} \) out of phase, with peaks at N4, N7, N11–N12, and N15. Only five residues fail to follow this pattern. Two of these residues, Gly and Ala, have very small sidechains and can therefore be accommodated on either side of the helix. Thr behaves similarly, being of intermediate polarity. As expected, Pro occurs very rarely except at the N1 position of helices.18 Histidine's failure to cluster either with the polar or non-polar probably reflects its \( \mathrm{p}K_{\mathrm{a}} \) near neutral \( \mathrm{pH} \).
