Two distinct groups were observed amongst the polar amino acids; the short polar amino acids show distributions different from those of the long polar amino acids near the N terminus. Such differences near the N terminus arise from specific hydrogen bonding patterns found near the ends of the helices.18 A second difference between Figure 2(a) and (b) is a shift in the overall distribution to a lower propensity for the shorter amino acids. Presumably, this reflects local hydrogen bond
interactions in the unfolded state that are more favorable for short polar residues due to a decreased entropic conformational penalty for forming such interactions. By contrast, the two groups of apolar residues showed similar patterns. Because the aromatic amino acids showed very little differentiation from other hydrophobic amino
acids, they are grouped together in subsequent Figures.
Further exploring the relationships between the plots of Figure 2, Figure 3 explores the average propensities of the residues in each physicochemical group. This averaging procedure further accentuates the periodicity observed in Figure 2,
and minimizes errors associated with counting statistics. Figure 3 shows propensity to be persistently periodic. To determine the persistence of the periodicity, an exponentially decaying sinusoid is fit to the data (Figure 3):
\[
\ln(P_{ij}(j))=y_0+A_0\mathrm{e}^{-j/l_p}\sin\left(\frac{2\uppi}{p}(j+x_0)\right)
\]
In performing the fit (Table 1), only positions N5–N15 were used, because the behavior near the N terminus is dominated by hydrogen binding to the exposed amide protons, and deviates from the distribution observed at the following positions. The fits to the three data sets were done simultaneously. Persistence length \( l_{p} \), period \( p \), and phase \( x_{0} \), were treated as global parameters, while \( y \)-offset \( y_{0} \) and amplitude \( A_{0} \) were allowed to vary from one physiochemical group to the next. In agreement with visual observation, we found \( p \) to be 3.63 residues. Remarkably, we also found a persistence length of 22 residues, indicating that
the sinusoidal behavior persists much longer than the length of most \( \alpha \)-helices (Figure 3).
Examination of Figure 3 suggests that there is a strong preference for hydrophobic residues to occur on the same side of the helix as N1. To further explore this possibility, we calculated the average hydrophobicity \( \overline{{\mathrm{Hb}_{j}}} \) at each position \( j \), using a scale \( \Delta G_{i}^{\mathrm{cor}} \) based on experimental free energies for transfer of each side-chain \( i \) from octanol to water:19
\[
\overline{\mathrm{Hb}_j}=\sum_i\Delta G_i^{\mathrm{cor}}\frac{n_{ij}}{n_j}
\]
The data were fit (Table 2) to an exponentially decaying sinusoid. Again, a periodic distribution with a repeat of 3.6 residues was observed. The amplitude (peak to trough) of the sine wave was \( 0.79\,\mathrm{kcal/mol} \) \( (1\,\mathrm{cal}=4.184\,\mathrm{J}) \), larger than the thermal energy at room temperature \( (0.58\,\mathrm{kcal/mol}) \). Periodicity of hydrophobicity within helices has been observed17,20–23 but not in relation to the
capping positions, nor with a comparably sized data set. Thus, although it has long been known that helices in globular proteins tend to be amphiphilic, it was not observed that the hydrophobic residues occurred with an equally well-defined phase relative to the start of the helix.
We conclude from Figure 4 that helices are more hydrophobic on the N1 side. Therefore, we explore whether helices tend to be more solvent-accessible on this side. Figure 5 shows the position-dependent, solvent-accessible area (ACC) determined by averaging DSSP ACC values across all residues that are a given distance from the N-cap.24
The fit in Figure 5 is again an exponentially decaying sinusoid. The accessibility (ACC) values
observed for N5–N14 (Figure 5) lend strong, support to the idea that the position of a residue determines whether it is likely to be buried or exposed. For example, \(\mathrm{ACC}_{\mathrm{N11}}\) is \(22\,\mathring{\mathrm{A}}^{2}\), while only two positions later, \(\mathrm{ACC}_{\mathrm{N13}}\) more than doubles to \(46\,\mathring{\mathrm{A}}^{2}\). While a relationship between accessibility and helical formation has been argued,25 the relationship between the accessibility of a residue and its distances from helical caps has, to our knowledge, gone thus far undetected, presumably because large datasets were unavailable during previous studies.
It is likely that the observed distribution of propensities and physical properties arises because the capping residue defines the relationship of a
helix to the solvent-accessible core of a protein. If this is the case, one might expect to observe similar \(\phi/\psi\) angles for the N-cap residues. Indeed, we observed that \(80\%\) of the helices in our database have the N-cap residue in the \(\beta\) region of the Ramachandran plot. This conformation is frequently observed because it allows efficient "N-capping" of the helices when the residues at the N-cap position are Ser, Thr, Asp, or Asn.9
Having observed a strong periodicity in helix propensity as a function of position from the N-cap, we next examined position-specific propensity as a function of the distance from the C-cap. Figures 6 and 7 show plots of amino acid propensities as a function of distance from the C-cap in helices of at least 15 residues. Figure 7 illustrates averages for each group. The periodicity of the propensities for polar residues is again apparent, although Ser tends to have a more isotropic distribution than in Figure 2. The periodicity for the aromatic amino acids is also less striking than in Figure 2. Also, the \(\beta\)-branched amino acids, Val and Ile, tend to be under-represented, possibly for steric reasons. We observe the same period and phase as for the N-cap plot, but a shorter correlation length of eight residues. The accessibility as a function of distance from the C-cap is plotted in Figure 8.
## Conclusion
The motivation for this study was to develop new guidelines for de novo protein design. Unexpectedly, we found that helical propensities are strongly position-dependent throughout the entire length of most helices, indicating that the protein chain tends to enter and exit from the solvent-inaccessible side of surface helices in proteins. We speculate that this orientation provides the most efficient sequestration of apolar core residues from water. Indeed, it has been shown that residues at the N1 position can interact hydrophobically with apolar side-chains at positions N3 and N4 of a helix.10,11 Similarly, such interactions occur frequently in C-capping motifs.2
## Methods
Helical residues were defined in terms of their \(\phi/\psi\)
angles. Numeric definitions for alpha left, alpha right, and extended came from Balaram.14 Alpha right conformations are defined as having \(\phi\) in the range of \(-140\) to \(-30\), with \(\psi\) between \(-90\) and 45. Extended turns are defined as \(\phi=(-180,-30)\) with \(\psi=(-180,-150)\) or (60,180). Alpha left residues are \(\phi=(20,125)\), \(\psi=(-45,90)\).
